---
import Content from '../layouts/Content.astro';
import { company, site, power } from '../data/site';

/* Derived from the published figures rather than retyped, so the arithmetic can
   never drift from the site record. */
const drawKw = Number(power.phase1aDraw.replace(/[^\d.]/g, ''));
const rateUsd = Number(power.rate.replace(/[^\d.]/g, ''));
const annualKwh = drawKw * 8760;
const annualCost = Math.round(annualKwh * rateUsd);

/* The two halves of the published queue position, so the headline figure and
   its qualifier can be set at different sizes without retyping either. */
const [queueAnswer = power.interconnectionQueue, queueBasis = ''] =
  power.interconnectionQueue.split('—').map((s) => s.trim());
const headroomFactor = power.headroom.split(' ')[0];
---

<Content
  title={`Power — behind the meter, no interconnection queue | ${company.full}`}
  description={`The industry interconnection queue runs ${power.industryQueue}. ${site.designation} never joined it: a ${power.transformer} service already on site at ${power.rate}, ${power.headroom}, plus ${power.solarPlanned} of solar and ${company.parent} LFP storage behind the meter.`}
  kicker="The power story"
  heading="Everyone else is in a queue. We never joined it."
  lede={`The average interconnection queue runs ${power.industryQueue}. GPUs stopped being the constraint some time ago; energised capacity is the constraint. ${site.designation} has a ${power.transformer} service standing on owned land, with ${power.headroom.toLowerCase()}.`}
  meta={[
    { label: 'Installed service', value: power.transformer, note: `${power.voltage}, energised on site`, tone: 'gold' },
    { label: 'Energy', value: power.rate, note: 'Published site rate' },
    { label: 'Headroom', value: headroomFactor, note: power.headroom, tone: 'signal' },
    { label: 'Interconnection queue', value: queueAnswer, note: power.interconnectionQueue },
  ]}
>
  <section aria-labelledby="queue" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">01</span><span class="eyebrow__t">The problem</span></p>
    <h2 id="queue">The queue is the whole industry's problem</h2>

    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p>
          A greenfield AI data center has to ask a utility for load. That request becomes
          a study: feasibility, system impact, facilities. Behind it sits a queue of
          generation and load requests on the same transmission system, and the position
          you take in that queue is the position you keep. The industry average runs
          {' '}<strong>{power.industryQueue}</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          Nothing about that timeline is fixed by having capital, having GPUs, or having
          a signed tenant. You can hold a purchase order for accelerators that ship in
          months and still be four years from energising them. That is the gap the market
          is currently paying a premium to close.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="versus" data-reveal>
      <div class="versus__side">
        <p class="versus__label">Industry interconnection queue</p>
        <p class="versus__value">{power.industryQueue}</p>
        <p class="versus__note">
          Time from filing a request to energised service. This is the number every other
          site on your shortlist is quietly working around.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="versus__side is-answer">
        <p class="versus__label">{site.designation} queue position</p>
        <p class="versus__value">{queueAnswer}</p>
        <p class="versus__note">
          {power.interconnectionQueue}. The {power.transformer} service is already
          energised on land we own{queueBasis ? `, and generation stays ${queueBasis}` : ''},
          so the application that would create a queue position was never required.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="btm" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">02</span><span class="eyebrow__t">Behind the meter</span></p>
    <h2 id="btm">Why {site.designation} is not in it</h2>

    <p>
      Two separate things put a project in a queue, and this site avoids both.
    </p>

    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>No new load request</h3>
        <p>
          The {power.transformer} service at {power.voltage} is already on the parcel and
          already energised. Phase 1A draws {power.phase1aDraw} against it —
          {' '}{power.phase1aUtilisation}. There is no new large-load application to file,
          so there is no study queue to enter.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>No generator interconnection</h3>
        <p>
          The planned {power.solarPlanned} of solar and the {company.parent} storage sit
          on our side of the utility meter and serve on-site load. Behind-the-meter
          generation that does not export is not a generator interconnection request. It
          is a load reduction.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Queue position</h3>
        <p>
          {power.interconnectionQueue}. Not "expedited", not "advanced" — absent, because
          the application that would create one was never required.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout" style="margin-top: var(--s-6);">
      <p>
        <strong>The one sentence that matters.</strong> Every competing site is selling you
        a date it has to be granted by somebody else. {site.designation} is selling you a
        fit-out schedule inside a building that already has power on it, targeting
        {' '}{site.powerOn}.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="service" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">03</span><span class="eyebrow__t">The service</span></p>
    <h2 id="service">What is standing on the parcel</h2>

    <dl class="spec">
      <div><dt>Transformer</dt><dd>{power.transformer}<span class="note">On site, energised.</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Distribution</dt><dd>{power.voltage}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Energy rate</dt><dd>{power.rate}<span class="note">The published rate for the site. Tenant pass-through terms are on the colocation page.</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Phase 1A draw</dt><dd>{power.phase1aDraw} IT load</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Service utilisation at Phase 1A</dt><dd>{power.phase1aUtilisation}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Headroom</dt><dd>{power.headroom}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Interconnection queue</dt><dd>{power.interconnectionQueue}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Utility of record</dt><dd><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: serving utility / cooperative name and account status]</span></dd></div>
    </dl>

    <blockquote class="pull">
      <p>{headroomFactor} headroom, already installed.</p>
      <p>
        Phase 1A consumes roughly a twentieth of what is standing. A tenant growing from
        a few racks to a few hundred kilowatts does not trigger a utility upgrade, a new
        study, or a new date — it consumes capacity that is already sitting there.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <figure class="photo" style="margin-top: var(--s-6);">
      <div class="photo__frame">
        <svg class="photo__icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="28" height="28" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" aria-hidden="true">
          <rect x="2.5" y="6" width="19" height="14" rx="2" />
          <circle cx="12" cy="13" r="3.5" />
          <path d="M8.5 6l1.4-2.2h4.2L15.5 6" />
          <path d="M3 3l18 18" />
        </svg>
        <p class="photo__state">Photograph not taken yet</p>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        <b>[PHOTO NEEDED]</b> The {power.transformer} transformer on its pad, and the
        service entrance into Building 1. For a buyer evaluating this page, this is the
        single most load-bearing photograph on the whole site — it is the proof that the
        power claim is not a plan.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="solar" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">04</span><span class="eyebrow__t">Generation and storage</span></p>
    <h2 id="solar">Solar and storage, on our side of the meter</h2>

    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p>
          {power.solarPlanned} of solar is planned on the parcel — there is no shortage of
          land for it on {site.acres} acres. Paired with it, {power.storage.toLowerCase()}.
        </p>
        <p>
          The storage is the part most operators cannot replicate cheaply. {company.parent}
          builds LFP cabinets; {company.name} is the data center arm of the same company.
          Storage lands on this site at manufacturer cost rather than at a system
          integrator's margin, which changes what is economically sensible to install.
        </p>
        <p>
          Behind the meter, that combination does three jobs: it shaves what the site
          draws through the utility service during the day, it holds energy for the hours
          it is worth more, and it puts real stored energy behind the load without an
          export arrangement or a queue position.
        </p>
      </div>

      <dl class="figures">
        <div>
          <dt>Solar, planned</dt>
          <dd class="is-gold">{power.solarPlanned}<span class="sub">On the parcel, behind the meter</span></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Storage</dt>
          <dd>LFP<span class="sub">{power.storage}</span></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Export to grid</dt>
          <dd>None<span class="sub">Serves on-site load, so no generator interconnection request</span></dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top: var(--s-6);">
      Sizing detail a buyer will want before modelling anything —
      {' '}<span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: battery cabinet count, usable kWh, C-rate and expected daily cycles]</span>,
      {' '}<span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: solar array configuration, tilt, expected annual yield in MWh]</span>,
      and <span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: construction and commissioning dates for the solar array and the battery plant]</span>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="economics" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">05</span><span class="eyebrow__t">Economics</span></p>
    <h2 id="economics">What the power actually costs</h2>

    <p>
      One published rate, {power.rate}, and one worked example so you can check our
      arithmetic rather than take a "competitive rates" claim on trust.
    </p>

    <div class="scroller" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Phase 1A energy arithmetic, scrollable">
      <table>
        <caption>Phase 1A energy, worked from published figures</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Line</th>
            <th scope="col">Figure</th>
            <th scope="col">Where it comes from</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">IT load</th>
            <td class="num">{power.phase1aDraw}</td>
            <td>Published Phase 1A draw</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Hours in a year</th>
            <td class="num">8,760</td>
            <td>Continuous operation, 100% utilisation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Annual energy</th>
            <td class="num">{annualKwh.toLocaleString()} kWh</td>
            <td>Load × hours</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Energy rate</th>
            <td class="num">{power.rate}</td>
            <td>Published site rate</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Annual energy cost</th>
            <td class="num">≈ ${annualCost.toLocaleString()}</td>
            <td>Energy × rate. IT load only.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Cooling and facility overhead</th>
            <td class="num"><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: measured PUE]</span></td>
            <td>Not modelled here. Direct-to-chip liquid cooling changes this materially; we will publish a measured number, not a design number.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top: var(--s-5);">
      This is arithmetic on two published numbers at full utilisation, for IT load only.
      It is not a quote, it is not a tenant price, and it does not include cooling
      overhead, network, or space. Tenant commercial terms are on the
      {' '}<a href="/colocation">colocation page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="is-end" aria-labelledby="unclaimed" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">06</span><span class="eyebrow__t">Not yet claimed</span></p>
    <h2 id="unclaimed">What we are not claiming about resilience</h2>

    <p>
      A behind-the-meter position solves schedule. It does not by itself make a claim
      about uptime, and we are not going to imply one. Every item below is a question a
      serious tenant asks in the first meeting, and every one of them is open.
    </p>

    <ul class="bullets">
      <li><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: UPS topology and ride-through duration for the IT load]</span></li>
      <li><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: standby generation — whether any is installed, fuel type, run time]</span></li>
      <li><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: electrical redundancy — N, N+1, or 2N, at the distribution and UPS levels]</span></li>
      <li><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: whether the site targets a formal resilience classification, and which]</span></li>
      <li><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: switchgear arrangement and single points of failure between the transformer and the racks]</span></li>
      <li><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: uptime SLA offered to tenants, and the service credit schedule behind it]</span></li>
      <li><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: maintenance windows and how they are notified]</span></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="/colocation">Colocation terms</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/site">See the site</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Content>
